<div class="section-eyebrow">The framing problem</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">Every Hormuz headline is about oil. The fertiliser story is real and it is not the same shape.</h2>

<p class="prose">Six months into the closure, the oil numbers are well covered: transit down to a few percent of normal, war risk premiums at multiples of the pre-crisis rate, Brent up more than twenty percent. What gets less attention is that the Gulf is also a major global source of urea and sulphur, both direct output and a byproduct of the region's oil and gas refining, and that trade has been disrupted alongside the oil story since the closure began on 28 February. India, Sri Lanka, and Thailand are the most exposed importers. This matters for food security in a direct way that the oil headlines do not: urea is fertiliser, and a fertiliser supply shock reaches a farmer's input costs the next planting season, not the next fill-up.</p>

<p class="prose">This piece was put together for a live segment on the Granular podcast with Holly Birkett, recorded 27 August 2026. The number that came out of preparing for it is more interesting than the one that went in.</p>

<div class="section-eyebrow">The correction</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">Oman does not transit the strait. Most Gulf dependency figures do not know that.</h2>

<p class="prose">The standard way to state Gulf fertiliser exposure is a single country-level number: India's urea imports are roughly 85% dependent on the Gulf. That number is not wrong as a description of where the cargo comes from. It is wrong as a description of what actually requires the Strait of Hormuz to stay open, because it silently includes Oman, and Oman's main fertiliser export terminal, Sur, sits on the Gulf of Oman coast, seaward of the strait. A vessel loading at Sur never enters the Persian Gulf and never needs Hormuz to reopen.</p>

<p class="prose">Oman is not a small share of that trade. India's OMIFCO joint venture, a dedicated bilateral supply arrangement between the two countries, makes Oman 57.8% of India's Gulf-sourced urea, by value, on 2024 Comtrade data, more than every other Gulf exporter combined. Correcting for that, India's actual Hormuz-specific urea dependency is closer to 36%, not 85%. Sri Lanka and Thailand have smaller Oman shares, 15.3% and 20.9% respectively, so the correction is smaller but still real: Sri Lanka moves from roughly 78% to roughly 66%, Thailand from roughly 67% to roughly 53%.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-label">Why this kind of correction matters, and why it generalises</div>
  <div class="callout-body">This is the same class of error the Narrows model has already caught and fixed for crude oil and LNG specifically, both of which had an explicit routing correction removing Oman's Hormuz attribution once it was noticed that Oman's loading terminals, Mina al-Fahal for crude and Qalhat for LNG, are also seaward of the strait. That fix was never extended to fertiliser, or to refined petroleum, LPG, and ammonia, all of which carry the same Gulf-wide dependency figure without the Oman correction applied. This page corrects fertiliser. The other three commodities carry the same open question and are flagged here rather than asserted either way until the same check has been run against them.</div>
</div>

<div class="section-eyebrow">The number the market and the physical geography disagree on</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">Oman is outside the strait. It is not outside the war risk zone.</h2>

<p class="prose">The physical correction above does not mean Oman-sourced cargo is priced as safe. The Joint War Committee's Listed Areas circular, JWLA-033, issued 3 March 2026, explicitly names Oman as a listed country, and by the circular's own definition, a listed country's ports and coastal waters out to 12 nautical miles are covered unless specifically varied. Oman has no variation. Sur, Sohar, Duqm, Salalah, and Muscat are all inside the current war risk boundary, which is drawn around the whole theatre, the Persian Gulf, Gulf of Oman, Arabian Sea, Gulf of Aden and Southern Red Sea, on regional threat exposure, not on which vessels physically transit a specific strait.</p>

<p class="prose">So the honest version of this holds two facts at once, and they point in different directions. Physically, Omani urea does not need Hormuz to reopen, the dependency correction above is real. Commercially, the market has not priced Oman as a safe alternative, because the war risk boundary was drawn on the region's overall threat exposure, missile and drone range, general Gulf of Oman risk, rather than on strait transit specifically. A cargo book that assumes Oman-sourced fertiliser is both physically and commercially insulated from the Hormuz crisis would be wrong on the second count even where it is right on the first.</p>

